  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.6 z²)/2, drawn at half height, steeper left-right. A step is learning rate × (slope + noise); steps along x grow without limit when the rate is above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83, so they diverge near the top of the range.",
    "Noise per direction has spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, the sampling-from-64-points rule: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. It is random and centred on the true slope, so a single step can raise the loss.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step (the way the ball will move). The grey arrow is the full-data step, learning rate × the true slope. Both are drawn 3× their true length. The step-length readout is the true length.",
    "A run ends after 40 updates (finished), or is diverged once the loss passes 5. Off-bowl positions are drawn at the bowl's edge and capped height; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "While playing, the ball makes one update every 5 host ticks (6 per second at speed 1). The trail holds the start plus up to 40 updates; the kept ghost holds one earlier run.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence: Reset replays it from the start; a new run uses the next values. Changing a setting mid-run applies from the next update."
  ]
}